{
	# Sites' on-demand TLS ask endpoint — must be the file's first block (Caddyfile global options).
	# The "ask" directive alone is not sufficient; each sites site block below still needs its own
	# tls { on_demand } to opt in (docs/research/dotnet-stack.md's Caddy section has the verified
	# syntax and why both pieces are required together).
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://api:8080/v1/sites/_ask-tls
	}
}

{$PRAXY_DOMAIN} {$PRAXY_CONSOLE_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}

# Every hosted site's public subdomain (<key>.<projectId>.{PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}). Two wildcard labels,
# not one — a site's hostname has *two* variable labels in front of the sites domain (key, then
# projectId), and Caddy's automation-policy subject matching is as strict about wildcard depth as a
# real TLS wildcard cert: `*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}` only matches a single label and silently refuses
# on-demand issuance (no ask call, no ACME attempt, an immediate TLS "internal_error" alert) for
# anything with an extra label in front — confirmed live against real Caddy with `debug` logging on,
# not theorized; see docs/research/dotnet-stack.md's Caddy section. Caddy does not need per-site
# awareness beyond this pattern — api dispatches Host -> container internally
# (Praxy.Sites.SiteProxyMiddleware) — so this is one static block regardless of how many sites exist.
# Each distinct hostname gets its own certificate issued lazily on first request, not one real
# wildcard cert (on-demand TLS's whole point: no DNS-01, no DNS-provider credentials).
#
# `issuer acme` is required, not optional — found live 2026-08-25, after the Sites Phase 3 catch-all
# block below started sharing this policy: Caddy's own default issuer selection is "internal if all
# subjects do not qualify for public certificates; otherwise acme and zerossl" (its own source,
# modules/caddytls/automation.go). A policy with no explicit `subjects` — which is what every
# `tls { on_demand }` block below collapses into once they're merged — can't be confirmed "public" by
# that heuristic, so Caddy silently issued self-signed *internal* certs for real site hostnames
# instead of real Let's Encrypt ones, with no error and no ask-endpoint call skipped (the ask call
# still happened; only the *issuer* silently downgraded). An explicit `issuer acme` bypasses that
# heuristic entirely. Verified live: without it, `caddy adapt`'s automation policy shows
# `"issuers":null` and a real on-demand request completes in <100ms with `"issuer":"local"` in the
# logs (far too fast for a real ACME round trip); with it, the policy shows an explicit acme issuer
# and a real request genuinely reaches `acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org` (confirmed via a throwaway
# instance — a fake `.test` hostname correctly got ACME's own "not a valid public suffix" rejection,
# proving a real attempt happened, not an instant internal fallback).
*.*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		on_demand
		issuer acme
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}

# A single deployment's preview URL (Sites Phase 2): <deploymentId>.<key>.<projectId>.{PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN}.
# Three wildcard labels, not two — same wildcard-depth strictness as the block above, confirmed by the
# same "against real Caddy, not pattern-matched" discipline the Phase 1 postmortem demanded: a
# throwaway Caddy instance with only the two-label block above got `"no certificate matching TLS
# ClientHello"` / `"on_demand":false` for a real three-label hostname, with the ask endpoint never
# called — reproducing Phase 1's exact bug shape one wildcard label short. Adding this dedicated block
# (an extra `*.`) made the same hostname correctly reach the ask endpoint. See
# docs/research/dotnet-stack.md's Caddy section for the full transcript.
#
# `issuer acme` here too — see the block above's comment for why; this block shares the same merged
# automation policy, so it needs the same explicit issuer to avoid the same silent internal-CA fallback.
*.*.*.{$PRAXY_SITES_DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		on_demand
		issuer acme
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}

# Custom domains a site owner points at this box (Sites Phase 3) — unlike the two blocks above, there
# is no fixed suffix or wildcard depth to match against; the whole point is arbitrary hostnames
# (`myapp.com`, `www.myapp.com`, ...) nobody configured in advance. `https://` with the host portion
# omitted is Caddy's own documented catch-all address (verbatim from caddyserver.com/on-demand-tls's
# own worked example for exactly this "many dynamically-created customer domains" shape) — it matches
# any hostname not claimed by a more specific site block. Placement in this file doesn't matter for
# routing: Caddy always picks the most specific matching address regardless of block order (its own
# docs, "If a request matches multiple site blocks, the site block with the most specific matching
# address is chosen") — confirmed live against real Caddy with all four blocks loaded together and
# `debug` logging on: a genuine 2-label site hostname and a genuine 3-label preview hostname each still
# triggered their own dedicated block's ask call, not this catch-all's, while both a legitimate
# unregistered custom domain and a made-up attacker-controlled hostname correctly fell through to this
# block's ask call instead (`api`'s _ask-tls, not Caddy, is what actually denies the attacker one). No
# shadowing of the console/API's own automatic-HTTPS block (`{$PRAXY_DOMAIN} {$PRAXY_CONSOLE_DOMAIN}`
# above) was observed either — its explicit hostnames form their own more-specific automation policy.
# See docs/research/dotnet-stack.md's Caddy section for the full transcript.
#
# `issuer acme` here too, for the same reason as the two blocks above — this bare, host-less address is
# in fact the block that *caused* the merged policy's `subjects` to end up empty in the first place, so
# it's the most important of the three to get right.
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
		issuer acme
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}
